<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  MAIN (and therefore RELEASE) network security policy: cleartext DENIED.

  This is the strict default. The debug source set overlays a permissive copy
  of this same resource name; release builds get this file, so a release APK
  cannot speak plain ws:// or http:// no matter what address the user enters.

  WHY CLEARTEXT IS NEEDED IN DEVELOPMENT AT ALL
  =============================================
  The existing backend serves an unencrypted WebSocket:

      backend/api/main.py  ==>  @app.websocket("/ws/video")
      run_backend.sh       ==>  uvicorn, bind 0.0.0.0, port 8000

  There is no TLS termination in front of it, so the uplink URL is
  ws://<laptop-ip>:8000/ws/video, which Android has blocked by default since
  API 28. The app targets API 37. Rather than weaken the whole app, cleartext
  is enabled ONLY in the debug build, and only for lab use on a private
  RFC 1918 Wi-Fi network between one phone and one laptop.

  WHY PRODUCTION MUST USE WSS
  ===========================
  Over ws:// the camera stream is unauthenticated and unencrypted: anyone on
  the same Wi-Fi can read the frames of the room being mapped, and nothing
  stops them connecting to /ws/video themselves and injecting frames into the
  depth pipeline. For any deployment outside a controlled lab the backend must
  terminate TLS and the app must connect over wss://, at which point this
  cleartext exception can be deleted rather than merely restricted.

  A NOTE ON SCOPING
  =================
  It would be preferable to permit cleartext only for the laptop's subnet.
  Android's Network Security Configuration does NOT support CIDR or subnet
  rules: the domain element accepts hostnames and literal IP addresses only.
  The backend address is also user-configurable at runtime and DHCP-assigned,
  so it cannot be enumerated here at build time. The honest description of the
  debug policy is therefore "cleartext allowed in debug builds", not
  "cleartext allowed for the local subnet"; the narrower claim would be false.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
</network-security-config>
